WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.740
Consider this four by four matrix.

00:00:02.680 --> 00:00:06.400
Put negative ones on the diagonal and positive ones everywhere else.

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:11.520
Its rows have a special property: every row is perpendicular to every

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:12.120
other row.

00:00:12.980 --> 00:00:14.440
Compare the first two rows.

00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:17.800
Two positions disagree and contribute negative one.

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:20.600
Two agree and contribute positive one.

00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:22.580
Their dot product is zero.

00:00:22.580 --> 00:00:27.640
Every row has squared length four, so H times H transpose equals

00:00:27.820 --> 00:00:28.900
four times the identity.

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:34.100
A matrix of signs with mutually orthogonal rows is called a Hadamard

00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:34.300
matrix.

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:39.740
In dimension n, its defining equation is H times H transpose equals

00:00:39.740 --> 00:00:41.220
n times the identity.

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:45.750
Hadamard matrices exist in dimensions one and two.

00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:50.150
In every larger dimension, the order must be divisible by four.

00:00:50.580 --> 00:00:55.140
The general Hadamard conjecture asks whether every positive multiple of four works.

00:00:56.180 --> 00:00:57.980
Our original matrix has an extra symmetry.

00:00:58.520 --> 00:01:01.740
Shift the first row cyclically one place to the right, and you

00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:02.480
get the second row.

00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:04.520
Repeat to get the third and fourth.

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:07.200
The entire matrix is determined by one row.

00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:09.920
Such a matrix is called circulant.

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:15.600
The circulant Hadamard conjecture says that this extra symmetry leaves only orders

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:16.640
one and four.

00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:20.400
We will explain the proposed proof through algebra and number theory.

00:01:21.020 --> 00:01:25.220
At this overview level, two deeper results are accepted inputs: the order

00:01:25.220 --> 00:01:27.020
restriction and the delta theorem.

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:30.340
The proof is organized into eight components.

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:34.160
Follow the arrows from each ingredient to the steps that use it.

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:38.700
First, group ring encoding replaces the first row by a polynomial.

00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:42.260
Multiplication by its cyclic reversal records every dot product.

00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:45.020
Orthogonality becomes one polynomial equation.

00:01:46.060 --> 00:01:50.320
Second, the order restriction says that, apart from order one, n is

00:01:50.460 --> 00:01:52.820
four times u squared, with u odd.

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:56.110
It remains to rule out u greater than one.

00:01:56.600 --> 00:02:00.880
Third, split the cyclic structure into a part of size four and

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:01.960
a part of odd size.

00:02:02.440 --> 00:02:07.240
Evaluating the resulting polynomial at one, negative one, and i produces three

00:02:07.240 --> 00:02:10.820
polynomials, c, d, and g, with the same norm identity.

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:15.300
Fourth, the delta theorem supplies the number theoretic input.

00:02:16.120 --> 00:02:19.560
A special product of values at roots of unity must itself be

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:21.040
a root of unity of odd order.

00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:24.040
The three polynomials satisfy its hypotheses.

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:27.560
Fifth, the original signs give another constraint.

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:31.500
Combine c, d, and g into a new polynomial, E.

00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:36.390
Modulo one plus i, every coefficient of E becomes one.

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:39.220
Sixth, bring the two branches together.

00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:43.700
Certain odd order roots of unity are also congruent to one modulo

00:02:43.860 --> 00:02:44.540
one plus i.

00:02:45.010 --> 00:02:48.860
Rigidity forces those roots to equal one, giving exact product relations.

00:02:50.180 --> 00:02:52.360
Seventh, turn those products into sums.

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:58.400
A first order expansion shows that a particular alternating sum, S, is

00:02:58.590 --> 00:03:00.680
zero modulo one plus i.

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:03.760
Eighth, use the all ones coefficients.

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:07.940
At nontrivial roots of unity their contributions cancel.

00:03:08.660 --> 00:03:12.780
At one, an odd number of ones survives, forcing that same sum

00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:14.740
to be one modulo one plus i.

00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:19.550
The same sum cannot be both zero and one in this nonzero

00:03:19.880 --> 00:03:20.420
quotient.

00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:21.820
That is the contradiction.

00:03:23.020 --> 00:03:24.990
Thus u cannot exceed one.

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:29.200
Together with the trivial case, the only possible orders are one and

00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:29.980
four.

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:33.540
